<type>: <subject>

# <type> is one of: feat | fix | perf | refactor | build | ci | docs | style | test | chore | revert
# <subject> is imperative, lower case, no trailing period, ≤72 chars total on this line.
#
# Body (optional): the *why*, wrapped at ~72 chars. Blank line above it.

# Footer (optional):
# BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and how to migrate>
# Closes #<issue>
#
# --- Why this format ---
# main only takes squash-merges (see DEPLOYMENT.md), and GitHub's squash
# commit message is the PR title — so it's the PR title that must be a
# Conventional Commit (CI lints it), not each commit on your branch. This
# template just gets you in the habit and keeps history readable in the
# meantime:
#   feat:     new feature                -> minor release
#   fix:      bug fix                    -> patch release
#   perf:     performance improvement    -> patch release
#   refactor/build/ci/docs/style/test/chore/revert -> no release
#   `!` after the type, or a BREAKING CHANGE: footer -> major release
# See README.md "Versioning and releases" and release.config.js.
